  <dataqual>
    <attracc>
      <attraccr>Statistics describe the CRMS 'adjusted' salinity, temperature and water elevation as published by CPRA; sensor gaps, relocations (several sites moved from -H01 to -H02 in 2013) and servicing were not corrected further, and the accuracy of the CRMS sensors is not restated here. A daily mean needs at least 12 hourly values and a monthly mean at least 15 valid days. The seasonal Mann-Kendall test used does not correct for serial correlation between years, so p values may be too small; the FDR adjustment does not fix that. Trend results depend on the window (full-record trends at Lake Maurepas are driven by high salinity in 2007 to 2012). Water-level trends depend on benchmark ties to NAVD88 and on river stage and storm years and are not sea-level rise rates.</attraccr>
    </attracc>
    <logic>One row per station, 33 unique station IDs; column order and names checked against the CSV header. All stations have a full-record trend, a 2013 to 2025 trend and a water-level trend (wl_start 2013-10-01 and wl_geoid GEOID12B at every station). peak_month and low_month are integers 1 to 12. The GeoJSON has 33 Point features whose coordinates equal the lat and lon properties, in OGC CRS84 (longitude, latitude) order. The CSV summary is written with 4 significant digits.</logic>
    <complete>Covers only the 33 selected stations, not all 436 CRMS hydro stations with salinity. Key sites were picked by a simple rule (evenly along latitude); another rule would give different stations. Trend series end between 2024-11-01 and 2025-12-01 because some records stop before December 2025. Data after 2025-12-31 are used only in the first, last and sal_days fields of the station layer.</complete>
    <posacc>
      <horizpa>
        <horizpar>Station coordinates are copied from the CPRA CIMS Data Collection Stations layer (projected to WGS 84) and were not checked in the field. The GeoJSON and data/crms_stations_selected.csv keep 5 decimal places; the summary CSV rounds to 0.01 degree, which moves stations by up to 641 m (median 451 m).</horizpar>
      </horizpa>
    </posacc>
